<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Our Lady of Vladimir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early 12th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Theotokos of Vladimir, Virgin of Vladimir</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Our Lady of Vladimir is a Byzantine icon of the Virgin and Child, painted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in the early twelfth century and sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> around 1130. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrei-bogolyubsky">Andrei Bogolyubsky</a> took it to Vladimir in 1155, and in 1395 it was brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, where it was credited with saving the city from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>. One of the most venerated images in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, it is now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tretyakov-gallery">Tretyakov Gallery</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/our-lady-of-vladimir.jpg" alt="Our Lady of Vladimir" width="320" height="466" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michel Bakni · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Virgin_of_Vladimir.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
